</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{CollectPreferences}(\Phi,\hat{\mathbf{T}},x_{m^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">CollectPreferences</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{CollectPreferences}(\Phi,\hat{\mathbf{T}},x_{m^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> VLM comparison
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\leftarrow\textsc{UpdatePosterior}(f,\mathcal{S}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">UpdatePosterior</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\leftarrow\textsc{UpdatePosterior}(f,\mathcal{S}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Prefers}(x_{m^{\star}},\hat{\mathbf{T}}\mid\mathcal{S}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prefers</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Prefers}(x_{m^{\star}},\hat{\mathbf{T}}\mid\mathcal{S}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{T}}\leftarrow x_{m^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{T}}\leftarrow x_{m^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}_{i,\mathrm{ref}}\leftarrow\hat{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}_{i,\mathrm{ref}}\leftarrow\hat{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ref}}\leftarrow\{\mathbf{T}_{i,\mathrm{ref}}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ref}}\leftarrow\{\mathbf{T}_{i,\mathrm{ref}}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the refinement module can optimize full 6-DoF poses—translation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and rotational <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>—in our aerial-filming setting we restrict rotation to the one-parameter subgroup about the vertical axis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The refinement pipeline is capable of optimizing over <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, however, in the practice of optimizing over drone shots, we choose to only optimize for yaw and set pitch and roll to practically zero. <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math> will be used to represent yaw only.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We take as input a set of candidate poses <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{T}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{T}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the exploratory video, where each pose <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is paired with a concise textual description <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> summarizing what that viewpoint is intended to capture. These candidates serve as seeds for downstream selection and refinement rather than final shots. In addition, we assume access to a reconstructed 3D scene <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts as a geometric and photometric proxy for both rendering and collision checking. In deployment, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced with <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gaussian Splatting</em>; in our experiments we approximate <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with a LiDAR-based reconstruction that fulfills the same role. All poses <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the scene <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are expressed in a consistent world frame so that we can synthesize views at candidate poses and verify feasibility (e.g., obstacle clearance) using the same representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We treat refinement as a preference-based Bayesian optimization loop, where the goal is to learn which nearby pose produces the most aesthetic view.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>GP Prior</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume each pose <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> has an unknown “aesthetic utility” <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. To model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> smoothly, we place a Gaussian Process (GP) prior <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\sim\mathcal{GP}(0,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\sim\mathcal{GP}(0,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and use a covariance that factorizes over rotation and translation.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\mathbf{T}_{i},\mathbf{T}_{j})=k_{\mathrm{rot}}(\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{q}_{j})\,k_{\mathrm{trans}}(\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{p}_{j})+\varepsilon\delta_{ij}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>rot</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\mathbf{T}_{i},\mathbf{T}_{j})=k_{\mathrm{rot}}(\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{q}_{j})\,k_{\mathrm{trans}}(\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{p}_{j})+\varepsilon\delta_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{rot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>rot</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{rot}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures similarity via the SO(3) geodesic between unit quaternions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{trans}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{trans}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a squared-exponential kernel on the Euclidean distance between positions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> initializes this prior once and then refines each seed locally.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Choosing the Next Pose</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=64</annotation></semantics></math> new pose candidates each iteration. Let the current incumbent be <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}=[\mathbf{p}_{\text{best}},\,\theta_{\text{best}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}=[\mathbf{p}_{\text{best}},\,\theta_{\text{best}}]</annotation></semantics></math> with trust–region radii <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho_{\mathrm{rot}},\rho_{\mathrm{trans}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>rot</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho_{\mathrm{rot}},\rho_{\mathrm{trans}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1,\dots,64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1,\dots,64</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\text{(rotational)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;\Delta\theta_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}\!\left[-\rho_{\mathrm{rot}},\,\rho_{\mathrm{rot}}\right],\\
&amp;\theta_{m}=\mathrm{wrap}\!\left(\theta_{\text{best}}+\Delta\theta_{m}\right),\\
&amp;\mathbf{q}_{m}=\theta_{m};\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>(rotational)</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.994em;" width="1.994em"><mi>Unif</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>rot</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>rot</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.913em;" width="1.913em"><mi>wrap</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\text{(rotational)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;\Delta\theta_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}\!\left[-\rho_{\mathrm{rot}},\,\rho_{\mathrm{rot}}\right],\\
&amp;\theta_{m}=\mathrm{wrap}\!\left(\theta_{\text{best}}+\Delta\theta_{m}\right),\\
&amp;\mathbf{q}_{m}=\theta_{m};\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\text{(translational)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;u_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(S^{2})\\
&amp;r_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}\!\left[0,\,\rho_{\mathrm{trans}}\right],\\
&amp;\delta\mathbf{p}_{m}=r_{m}\,u_{m},\\
&amp;\mathbf{p}_{m}=\mathrm{clamp}_{z}\!left(\mathbf{p}_{\text{best}}+\delta\mathbf{p}_{m});\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>(translational)</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.994em;" width="1.994em"><mi>Unif</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:2.491em;" width="2.491em"><mi>clamp</mi></mpadded><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\text{(translational)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;u_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(S^{2})\\
&amp;r_{m}\sim\mathrm{Unif}\!\left[0,\,\rho_{\mathrm{trans}}\right],\\
&amp;\delta\mathbf{p}_{m}=r_{m}\,u_{m},\\
&amp;\mathbf{p}_{m}=\mathrm{clamp}_{z}\!left(\mathbf{p}_{\text{best}}+\delta\mathbf{p}_{m});\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\text{(candidate)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;x_{m}=(\mathbf{p}_{m},\,\mathbf{q}_{m})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>(candidate)</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\text{(candidate)}\,\,\,\,\,\,&amp;x_{m}=(\mathbf{p}_{m},\,\mathbf{q}_{m})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As outlined in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we select a challenger <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> using Dueling Thompson Sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu,\Sigma)\quad\text{(posterior over }\{\hat{T}\}\cup X_{t}\text{)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>(posterior over </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu,\Sigma)\quad\text{(posterior over }\{\hat{T}\}\cup X_{t}\text{)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{m}\;=\;\tilde{f}(x_{m})\;-\;\tilde{f}(\hat{\mathbf{T}}),\qquad m=1,\ldots,M," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{m}\;=\;\tilde{f}(x_{m})\;-\;\tilde{f}(\hat{\mathbf{T}}),\qquad m=1,\ldots,M,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}\;=\;\arg\max_{m}a_{m},\qquad\text{challenger }=\ x_{m^{\star}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>m</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>challenger </mtext><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}\;=\;\arg\max_{m}a_{m},\qquad\text{challenger }=\ x_{m^{\star}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16176v1/results-6.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative rollouts across three scenes.</span> Columns correspond to simulated scenes—(left) <em id="S3.F4.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cottage</em>, (middle) <em id="S3.F4.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">restaurant</em>, (right) <em id="S3.F4.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">luxury</em>. For each scene, we show two ACDC trajectories generated from the prompt shown above; three representative keyframes per trajectory are displayed, and the pair of path overlays for the scene appears at the bottom.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Preference Feedback</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We render the incumbent <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a challenger <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>, present the two views with the instruction to a vision–language model, and record a single binary comparison <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Algorithm 1 this label is stored in the per-iteration comparison set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The comparison is encoded with the Bradley–Terry likelihood</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(s_{ij}\mid f_{i},f_{j})=\sigma\!\big(s_{ij}\,(f_{i}-f_{j})\big),\qquad s_{ij}\in\{+1,-1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(s_{ij}\mid f_{i},f_{j})=\sigma\!\big(s_{ij}\,(f_{i}-f_{j})\big),\qquad s_{ij}\in\{+1,-1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which links the probability of choosing one view to the latent utility difference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>4 </span>Update Posterior</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each new comparison updates the GP, giving us a posterior distribution over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{T})</annotation></semantics></math> indicating current best estimate over ”aesthetic utility” scores over regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Iteratively, this loop steadily improves the estimate of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{T})</annotation></semantics></math> and converges to a refined pose that better aligns with the user’s intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The refinement stage produces a set of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> refined poses:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\text{ref}}=\{\mathbf{T}_{k,\text{ref}}\}_{k=1}^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\text{ref}}=\{\mathbf{T}_{k,\text{ref}}\}_{k=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">that provide improved framing for the user’s prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Trajectory Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given refined waypoints <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\text{ref}}=\{\mathbf{T}_{k,\text{ref}}\}_{k=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\text{ref}}=\{\mathbf{T}_{k,\text{ref}}\}_{k=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with positions <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{k,\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{k,\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and yaws <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k,\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k,\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a voxel traversability map <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we produce a time-parameterized, collision-free trajectory in three concise steps.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>1 </span>Path Extraction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We connect the waypoint positions in order using <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{A}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{A}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on the free-voxel graph of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding a collision-free polyline</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{\mathbf{c}_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L},\quad\mathbf{c}_{1}=\mathbf{p}_{1,\text{ref}},\;\mathbf{c}_{L}=\mathbf{p}_{n,\text{ref}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{\mathbf{c}_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L},\quad\mathbf{c}_{1}=\mathbf{p}_{1,\text{ref}},\;\mathbf{c}_{L}=\mathbf{p}_{n,\text{ref}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">that visits every <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{k,\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{k,\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> sequentially.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>2 </span>Rectangular-Tunnel Corridor</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each segment <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{c}_{\ell},\mathbf{c}_{\ell+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{c}_{\ell},\mathbf{c}_{\ell+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unit tangent, choose orthonormal <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{n}_{\ell},\mathbf{b}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐧</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{n}_{\ell},\mathbf{b}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> spanning the orthogonal plane, and set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\ell}=\|\mathbf{c}_{\ell+1}-\mathbf{c}_{\ell}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\ell}=\|\mathbf{c}_{\ell+1}-\mathbf{c}_{\ell}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. We grow a rectangle centered on the segment (leaving margin <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> via a distance transform) to obtain cross-section <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{\ell},h_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{\ell},h_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. The box <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of points whose local coordinates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{b},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{b},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in the frame <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{n}_{\ell},\mathbf{b}_{\ell},\mathbf{t}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐧</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{n}_{\ell},\mathbf{b}_{\ell},\mathbf{t}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{n}|\leq\tfrac{w_{\ell}}{2},\quad|x_{b}|\leq\tfrac{h_{\ell}}{2},\quad|x_{t}|\leq\tfrac{\Delta_{\ell}}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{n}|\leq\tfrac{w_{\ell}}{2},\quad|x_{b}|\leq\tfrac{h_{\ell}}{2},\quad|x_{t}|\leq\tfrac{\Delta_{\ell}}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The corridor is <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{\mathcal{B}_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{\mathcal{B}_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; any curve that stays inside <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is collision-free.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>3 </span>Time-Optimal Trajectory Planning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Treat each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> as an oriented gate and use the Time-Optimal Gate-Traversing (TOGT) planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> to compute a continuous, piecewise-polynomial position trajectory that <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">remains</em> inside <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visits</em> all waypoints, and <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">respects</em> quadrotor dynamic limits. Since TOGT does not constrain yaw, let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the times at which <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> passes the refined waypoints. Unwrap <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\theta_{k,\text{ref}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\theta_{k,\text{ref}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and fit a <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> cubic spline <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(t)</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\tau_{k},\theta_{k,\text{ref}})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\tau_{k},\theta_{k,\text{ref}})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which results in a trajectory with smooth pans.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We intend to investigate the following questions through our experimentation:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language-to-shot quality.</span> Does ACDC translate natural-language prompts—ranging from high-level intent to concrete camera directives—into cinematically pleasing, prompt-aligned shots?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-scene generalization.</span> Can ACDC be generalized across a variety of scenes with drastically different visual features?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world executability.</span> Are ACDC-generated trajectories safe and dynamically feasible to execute on physical platforms, while remaining faithful to the planned camera motion?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both initial waypoint selection (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Initial Waypoint Selection ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>) and pose refinement (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>), we use Gemini 2.5 Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> as the vision–language model and AM-RADIO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> with a SigLIP 2 adaptor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> as the pretrained visual encoder. During refinement, each waypoint is optimized for 100 iterations within a trust region of 1m (translation) and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> (yaw).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ACDC trajectories are executed on hardware using an autopilot stack with a nonlinear Model Predictive Contouring Controller (MPCC). The controller robustly tracks aggressive, yaw-coupled motions; our yaw-aware planning in trajectory generation yields dynamically feasible paths that MPCC executes with low tracking error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a photorealistic simulator from a selection of indoor scenes from the HM3D dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and used Unity as our rendering engine. To obtain authentic exploration video as if the user is actually capturing a video for 3D reconstruction in the real world, we constructed a makeshift Virtual Reality (VR) setup utilizing a motion capture system that can actively track the pose of a handheld device mimicking a camera. The handheld device will display the rendered camera image in the simulator given the pose in real time. Because the mocap volume is limited to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 6\times 2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 6\times 2.5</annotation></semantics></math>m, we introduce a navigation mixer: x-y translation is commanded via a joystick, while z and orientation are taken from mocap, enabling natural exploration of scenes larger than the tracking volume.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qualitative Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present six ACDC rollouts across three distinct scenes (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B2 Choosing the Next Pose ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Prompts span (i) fine-grained instruction following, (ii) situation-aware reasoning, and (iii) abstract vibe-level intent. In the second row of the cottage example, ACDC follows step-by-step instructions with unambiguous referential cues. For situation-conditioned prompts, it targets task-relevant subspaces: in the restaurant, it tours the dining area for a “customer” request and prioritizes the kitchen for a “food inspector.” Under vibe-level prompts (e.g., the second luxury example), overall path coverage is similar to a full tour, but at the shot level ACDC selects characteristic viewpoints and keyframes that convey the intended mood.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16176v1/heatmap.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/477;" width="476" height="477" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>The user-provided exploration video and instruction are first processed by a large language model into <math id="S4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> waypoint descriptions. These descriptions are then converted into embeddings by a vision foundation model. Finally, the large language model selects <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> waypoint trios.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the frame-selection stage, we compute—for every frame in the exploration video—the cosine similarity between its visual embedding and the text embedding of the selected waypoint description (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Initial Waypoint Selection ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>). We project frame locations onto the floor plan and visualize the per-location scores as a heatmap (Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Qualitative Results ‣ IV Experiment ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Warm regions align with the intended area (e.g., the dining room), and the insets show the highest-scoring frames. These results indicate that retrieval (frame selection) reliably localizes the region of interest and provides strong initializations for subsequent pose refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pose Refinement Robustness Studies</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16176v1/pr-conv.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/261;" width="476" height="261" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pose-refinement robustness.</span> Cosine similarity between the current view and the reference view at <math id="S4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> over 100 iterations for seven random initializations within <math id="S4.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\,\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\,\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. All trials rapidly converge toward <math id="S4.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating reliable alignment to the target viewpoint. Thumbnails show the view before refinement (“Before”) and after refinement (“After”).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the robustness of our pose-refinement method, we find a waypoint
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\star}\in\mathrm{SE}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\star}\in\mathrm{SE}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> and randomly sample seven initial poses <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{T}^{(k)}_{0}\}_{k=1}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>7</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{T}^{(k)}_{0}\}_{k=1}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> within
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\,\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\,\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the large language model generated description <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, we run refinement for each of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{T}^{(k)}_{0}\}_{k=1}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>7</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{T}^{(k)}_{0}\}_{k=1}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> for 100 iterations. At each iteration, we render image <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given pose <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and acquire image embedding of current refinement pose each iteration <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}_{t}=\phi_{\mathrm{img}}(I_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐢</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>img</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}_{t}=\phi_{\mathrm{img}}(I_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We also embed <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathrm{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>img</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathrm{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> to get embedding <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}_{t}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐢</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}_{t}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and record the cosine similarity between <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}_{t}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐢</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}_{t}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐢</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at each iteration. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-C Pose Refinement Robustness Studies ‣ IV Experiment ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, all seven runs converges to a cosine similarity of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!1</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. Notably, the randomized initializations—including arbitrary yaw—thus create cases where the target object is initially out of view, yet still converge within our standard 100-iteration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hardware in the Loop Validation</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16176v1/hitl_numbered.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/385;" width="476" height="385" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware-in-the-loop experiment.</span> <em id="S4.F7.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bottom-left:</em> the orange path shows experimental drone trajectory visualized in a reconstructed kitchen. <em id="S4.F7.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right:</em> first-person views associated with each waypoint. <em id="S4.F7.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Top-left:</em> the experimental trajectory flaw by a real quadrotor. <em id="S4.F7.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inset:</em> the quadrotor used.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To verify that our simulated quadrotor dynamics faithfully reflect reality, we conducted hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) tests on the platform shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-D Hardware in the Loop Validation ‣ IV Experiment ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. The vehicle carries an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX running the autopilot stack.
External pose estimates from a motion-capture system are fused with onboard IMU measurements to produce full 6-DoF odometry. The quadrotor platform is able to complete the flight within centimeter-level difference to simulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User Studies</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated prompt–video alignment with a preference-based study: 41 participants rated, on a 1–5 scale (higher is better), how well each of the six outputs in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B2 Choosing the Next Pose ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> matches its prompt. We report mean in Fig <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-E User Studies ‣ IV Experiment ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The ordering of scenes and videos follows their left-to-right layout in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B2 Choosing the Next Pose ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16176v1/ratings_comparison.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/209;" width="476" height="209"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span><span id="S4.F8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User study ratings.</span> Mean 1–5 Likert scores (higher is better) for prompt–video alignment across six outputs: <em id="S4.F8.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cottage</em> (Left/Right), <em id="S4.F8.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Restaurant</em> (Left/Right), and <em id="S4.F8.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Luxury</em> (Left/Right), ordered as in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B2 Choosing the Next Pose ‣ III-B Pose Refinement ‣ III Methodology ‣ Agentic Aerial Cinematography:From Dialogue Cues to Cinematic Trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Bars show independent participant ratings from <math id="S4.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math> users (author of prompt self-ratings shown for reference), indicating medium-high prompt–video alignment across different scenes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than benchmarking against a single expert trajectory, we adopt preference-based evaluation because open-ended prompts admit multiple valid flights. Even expert-designed paths can differ substantially in trajectory distance while both satisfy intent and aesthetic criteria, making point-wise and path-wise distances to a fixed reference largely uninformative. By contrast, preference ratings directly measure the construct of interest—prompt–video alignment and perceived cinematographic quality—without relying on arbitrary geometric surrogates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced ACDC, a language-conditioned autonomous cinematography system that bridges abstract human intent and executable UAV trajectories. By combining semantic retrieval, preference-based optimization, and safe, dynamically feasible trajectory generation, ACDC enables non-experts to produce professional-grade indoor drone videos from natural-language prompts. Experiments in photorealistic simulation, hardware-in-the-loop testing, and user studies show that ACDC is robust to prompt abstraction, generalizes across diverse indoor scenes, and executes reliably on physical hardware. For future work, we aim to demonstrate an end-to-end, fully on-board system that performs 3D reconstruction from exploratory video, real-time localization and collision avoidance to capture cinematic video in the real world.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgment</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors thank Wenda Zhao for experimental support and H S Helson Go for his contribution in the development of the autopilot stack.</p>
</div>
</section>
